#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100652 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bd8d346-74cd-5ecf-86c1-b5a78ea3f6db" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Perimeter of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "e59d157f-d54a-565f-a2ab-fcf37f52cb1f" :question/math-academy-id "e-355" :question/problem "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semi-circle of radius $1$. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/00/0082f0e8263af8c4c17f514f577efc7605fa7178a6fc3842cefd48d4f4cd3d74.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. Since the hypotenuse of the triangle has length $2$, then by the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb^{2} &= (2)^{2} - l^{2}⟹b = \\sqrt{4 - l^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\nP(l) = 2 + l + \\sqrt{4-l^2}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $2$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0< l <2$.\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l)$, so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(l) &= 1 + \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{4 - l^{2}}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{4 - l^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $P'(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{′}(l) &= 0 \\\\\n1 - \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{4 - l^{2}}} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{4 - l^{2}}} &= 1 \\\\\nl &= \\sqrt{4 - l^{2}} \\\\\nl^{2} &= 4 - l^{2} \\\\\nl^{2} &= 2 \\\\\nl &= \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that\n\n$$\nl = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\ndoes indeed give a maximum value of $P(l)$. To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0, \\sqrt2)$ | $\\sqrt2$ | $(\\sqrt2,2)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $1.5$ |\n| $P'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $P$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nSo\n\n$$\nl = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\ndoes indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\sqrt{2}) &= 2 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{4 - (\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\\n&= 2 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{4 - 2} \\\\\n&= 2 + \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2} \\\\\n&= 2 + 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100657 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c35dab9-cf1e-5ae8-a9d0-e4f8b2703189" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Rectangle Inscribed in the First Quadrant Under a Given Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "da65d6c2-b59c-56cd-8db0-fd9d7af16c78" :question/math-academy-id "e-356" :question/problem "Find the area of the largest rectangle that can be inscribed in the closed region bounded by the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the graph of $y=f(x)=4 - \\dfrac{4}{3} x$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a diagram of the situation:\n\n![](images/54/5418d6b54ee048f36737d0a62cc8a8c1a1086e10c3d52595d29e2cf88a0aab74.png)\n\nLet $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x$, the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve\n\n$$\ny= 4 - \\dfrac{4}{3} x\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA = xy = x \\left(4 - \\dfrac{4}{3} x \\right) = 4x - \\dfrac{4}{3} x^2\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\nA'(x) = 4 - \\dfrac{8}{3} x\n$$\n\nand solving $A'(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{′}(x) &= 0 \\\\\n4 - \\frac{8}{3}x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{8}{3}x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\ndoes indeed give a maximum value of $A(x)$. To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $\\left(0,\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)$ | $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ | $\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2},3\\right)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $2$ |\n| $P'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $P$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nSo\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\ndoes indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(\\frac{3}{2}) &= 4(\\frac{3}{2}) - \\frac{4}{3}(\\frac{3}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= 6 - \\frac{4}{3}(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\\n&= 6 - 3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100782 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cccebbd-5c63-5284-a6b1-eaba281612f4" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "9bdb6bbf-0f6d-577d-934b-e861c249fdc0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7453" :question/problem "Consider a right triangle inscribed in a semi-circle of radius $3\\sqrt{2}$. Find the maximum possible area of the triangle." :question/worked-solution "We first draw a picture of the situation:\n\n![](images/02/02cb611fc10d1b81242fc56006ad1ee7a87f466d0432c4972193e8920fc80fa3.png)\n\nLet's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{72-l^2}$.\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l) = \\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{72-l^2} \\,\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $6\\sqrt{2}$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0< l< 6\\sqrt{2}$.\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l)$, so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{′}(l) &= \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}} - \\frac{l^{2}}{2\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $A'(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{′}(l) &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}} - \\frac{l^{2}}{2\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}}} &= 0 \\\\\n2\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}} - \\frac{l^{2}}{2\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}}}) &= 2\\sqrt{72 - l^{2}} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n72 - l^{2} - l^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nl^{2} &= \\frac{72}{2} \\\\\nl^{2} &= 36 \\\\\nl &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=6$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l)$. To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,6)$ | $6$ | $(6, 6\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $7$ |\n| $P'$ | $\\color{red}+$ | $0$ | $\\color{blue}-$ |\n| $P$ | $\\color{red}\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{max}$ | $\\color{blue}\\searrow$ |\n\nSo $l=6$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(6) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 \\cdot \\sqrt{72 - (6)^{2}} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\sqrt{72 - 36} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot \\sqrt{36} \\\\\n&= 3 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8cccebbd-5c63-5284-a6b1-eaba281612f4" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing the Area of a Triangle Inscribed in a Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b18b4495-0b50-5ca9-9a32-0b6a08574c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098710} {:answer/id #uuid "ce6730a8-f279-5ec5-a054-37e5abc434b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098711} {:answer/id #uuid "d070998c-3697-5d61-8e50-bcaaec161325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098712} {:answer/id #uuid "1b8d0338-3535-5e7b-912c-c06d9f7b2531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098713} {:answer/id #uuid "713961ac-76af-58c8-98c0-16d9dfde1aea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098714}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4d722e34-740b-5656-bc38-3aa54dc6255c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098709}] :question/id #uuid "0ff40911-3696-584e-9222-ff465336ddb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-16202" :question/problem "![](images/56/5688d71233361866c74b862470230bae26d3fb01d5beeb95f230f24d9dc462a3.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semi-circle of radius $2.5$, as shown above. Find the maximum possible area of the triangle.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
